{"name":"Flax","hex":"#eedc82","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Flax","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Flax"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flax","blocks":[{"text":"Flax, also known as common flax or line, is a flowering plant, Linum usitatissimum, in the family Linaceae. It is cultivated as a food and fiber crop in regions of the world with temperate climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_16%29_BHL303594.jpg/330px-K%C3%B6hler%27s_Medizinal-Pflanzen_in_naturgetreuen_Abbildungen_mit_kurz_erl%C3%A4uterndem_Texte_%28Plate_16%29_BHL303594.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flax","wikidata":"Q45108"},{"title":"Flax (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Flax or flaxen is a pale yellowish-gray, the color of straw or unspun dressed flax. The first recorded use of flax as a color name in English was in 1915, but flaxen had been used to describe hair color in David Copperfield, by Charles Dickens in 1849: Mr. Omer's granddaughter, Minnie, is described as \"a pretty little girl with long, flaxen, curling hair.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Vlasvezel.JPG/330px-Vlasvezel.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flax_(color)","wikidata":"Q3746538"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Flax_flowers.jpg/500px-Flax_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Flax_flowers.jpg/960px-Flax_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/F